1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,623
Sweetheart, when I was a little girl,

2
00:00:02,623 --> 00:00:05,247
the best part of Christmas wasn't the

3
00:00:05,247 --> 00:00:05,886
presents.

4
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:08,589
It was walking home from church on

5
00:00:08,589 --> 00:00:11,334
Christmas Eve, down Elm Street in the

6
00:00:11,334 --> 00:00:14,228
snow, looking into everybody's windows.

7
00:00:14,408 --> 00:00:16,880
Every window was a little picture.

8
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:18,222
A candle here,

9
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:20,131
paper snowflakes there,

10
00:00:20,131 --> 00:00:22,703
a tiny village in the next one.

11
00:00:22,883 --> 00:00:26,302
Today, most windows on most streets are

12
00:00:26,302 --> 00:00:27,179
just dark.

13
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:28,980
And it's so easy,

14
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:30,220
and so cheap,

15
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:30,888
to fix.

16
00:00:31,238 --> 00:00:33,981
So tonight I'm giving you nine Christmas

17
00:00:33,981 --> 00:00:36,656
window ideas that look magical from the

18
00:00:36,656 --> 00:00:38,714
street, for under ten dollars,

19
00:00:38,714 --> 00:00:41,458
and the three mistakes that make windows

20
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:43,173
look cluttered and cheap.

21
00:00:43,353 --> 00:00:44,531
And stay with me,

22
00:00:44,531 --> 00:00:47,028
because number nine is the window my

23
00:00:47,028 --> 00:00:48,623
mother made every year,

24
00:00:48,623 --> 00:00:50,980
and people used to slow their cars

25
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:53,130
down on Elm Street just to look

26
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:53,546
at it.

27
00:00:53,897 --> 00:00:56,330
Here's what nobody tells you,

28
00:00:56,330 --> 00:00:56,834
honey.

29
00:00:57,014 --> 00:01:00,001
Your window is the only decoration that

30
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:01,379
two audiences see.

31
00:01:01,559 --> 00:01:04,195
The family inside sees it all day.

32
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:05,796
And every neighbor,

33
00:01:05,796 --> 00:01:09,088
every mailman, every lonely person walking a

34
00:01:09,088 --> 00:01:11,258
dog at night sees it from the

35
00:01:11,258 --> 00:01:11,782
street.

36
00:01:11,962 --> 00:01:13,307
In my mother's day,

37
00:01:13,307 --> 00:01:15,856
a decorated window was a little gift

38
00:01:15,856 --> 00:01:17,697
to the whole neighborhood.

39
00:01:17,877 --> 00:01:18,973
You were saying,

40
00:01:18,973 --> 00:01:20,547
there's warmth in here.

41
00:01:20,727 --> 00:01:23,375
Merry Christmas, whoever you are.

42
00:01:23,724 --> 00:01:25,333
And windows are cheap,

43
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:28,405
sweetheart, because the dark does half the

44
00:01:28,405 --> 00:01:29,355
work for you.

45
00:01:29,535 --> 00:01:31,603
One candle in a window at night

46
00:01:31,603 --> 00:01:34,405
looks more magical than a thousand dollars

47
00:01:34,405 --> 00:01:36,873
of decorations on a lawn in daylight.

48
00:01:37,053 --> 00:01:39,031
In a minute I'll tell you the

49
00:01:39,031 --> 00:01:42,101
biggest mistake people make with that candle,

50
00:01:42,101 --> 00:01:44,284
because I see it on almost every

51
00:01:44,284 --> 00:01:44,762
street.

52
00:01:45,111 --> 00:01:46,325
Before you start,

53
00:01:46,325 --> 00:01:49,181
honey, go outside tonight after dark and

54
00:01:49,181 --> 00:01:51,823
stand across the street from your own

55
00:01:51,823 --> 00:01:52,252
house.

56
00:01:52,432 --> 00:01:54,288
Look at it the way a stranger

57
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:54,672
would.

58
00:01:54,852 --> 00:01:57,755
Which windows can you actually see?

59
00:01:57,935 --> 00:02:00,522
Which ones are hidden by bushes or

60
00:02:00,522 --> 00:02:01,511
a porch roof?

61
00:02:01,691 --> 00:02:03,572
That's where you put your effort.

62
00:02:03,752 --> 00:02:05,888
My mother did this every year on

63
00:02:05,888 --> 00:02:07,356
the first of December,

64
00:02:07,356 --> 00:02:09,025
in her coat and slippers,

65
00:02:09,025 --> 00:02:11,696
and the neighbors thought she'd lost her

66
00:02:11,696 --> 00:02:12,029
mind.

67
00:02:12,379 --> 00:02:15,558
Number one, a single candle in every

68
00:02:15,558 --> 00:02:16,176
window.

69
00:02:16,356 --> 00:02:19,949
This is the oldest American window tradition

70
00:02:19,949 --> 00:02:21,255
there is, honey.

71
00:02:21,435 --> 00:02:23,551
The old story goes that a candle

72
00:02:23,551 --> 00:02:26,394
in the window meant travelers were welcome,

73
00:02:26,394 --> 00:02:28,708
and that the family was waiting for

74
00:02:28,708 --> 00:02:30,096
someone to come home.

75
00:02:30,276 --> 00:02:33,288
Today, use the battery candles with a

76
00:02:33,288 --> 00:02:35,730
timer, so they come on at dusk

77
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:37,521
and go off at bedtime.

78
00:02:37,701 --> 00:02:39,348
One in every window,

79
00:02:39,348 --> 00:02:40,584
on every floor,

80
00:02:40,584 --> 00:02:42,066
facing the street.

81
00:02:42,246 --> 00:02:43,953
Simple, and it's stunning.

82
00:02:44,302 --> 00:02:47,251
Number two, paper snowflakes.

83
00:02:47,431 --> 00:02:50,144
A stack of plain white printer paper,

84
00:02:50,144 --> 00:02:51,538
a pair of scissors,

85
00:02:51,538 --> 00:02:54,397
and an evening with your grandchildren.

86
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:56,945
Fold the paper into a triangle,

87
00:02:56,945 --> 00:02:58,397
then in half again,

88
00:02:58,397 --> 00:03:01,376
then again, cut little shapes along the

89
00:03:01,376 --> 00:03:02,751
edges, and unfold.

90
00:03:02,931 --> 00:03:04,993
Every one is different,

91
00:03:04,993 --> 00:03:06,785
just like real ones.

92
00:03:06,965 --> 00:03:08,731
Tape them to the glass with a

93
00:03:08,731 --> 00:03:10,193
dot of tape on the back.

94
00:03:10,373 --> 00:03:12,160
From the street at night,

95
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,232
with the lamp on behind them,

96
00:03:14,232 --> 00:03:14,947
they glow.

97
00:03:15,297 --> 00:03:18,254
Number three, a wreath hung inside the

98
00:03:18,254 --> 00:03:20,510
window, on a long red ribbon.

99
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:23,345
Most people put the wreath on the

100
00:03:23,345 --> 00:03:26,402
door, where nobody sees it after dark.

101
00:03:26,582 --> 00:03:29,087
Hang one inside the front window,

102
00:03:29,087 --> 00:03:31,744
centered, from a ribbon tied to the

103
00:03:31,744 --> 00:03:32,654
curtain rod.

104
00:03:32,834 --> 00:03:34,405
With a lamp behind it,

105
00:03:34,405 --> 00:03:36,904
the wreath turns into a dark circle

106
00:03:36,904 --> 00:03:39,046
with light pouring through it.

107
00:03:39,226 --> 00:03:41,731
My mother made hers from the same

108
00:03:41,731 --> 00:03:44,996
pine branches she cut for her centerpieces.

109
00:03:45,345 --> 00:03:47,988
If Grandma's tips are helping you,

110
00:03:47,988 --> 00:03:50,087
tap the like button for me,

111
00:03:50,087 --> 00:03:50,941
sweetheart.

112
00:03:51,121 --> 00:03:54,454
It helps more people find these videos.

113
00:03:54,634 --> 00:03:58,233
Number four, glass ornaments on fishing line.

114
00:03:58,413 --> 00:04:00,273
Take a few old glass balls,

115
00:04:00,273 --> 00:04:02,134
tie each one to a length of

116
00:04:02,134 --> 00:04:03,443
clear fishing line,

117
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:05,442
and hang them from the top of

118
00:04:05,442 --> 00:04:08,060
the window frame at different heights.

119
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:09,326
From the street,

120
00:04:09,326 --> 00:04:11,905
they look like they're floating in the

121
00:04:11,905 --> 00:04:12,176
air.

122
00:04:12,356 --> 00:04:14,090
Use odd numbers,

123
00:04:14,090 --> 00:04:15,607
five or seven,

124
00:04:15,607 --> 00:04:17,558
and mix the sizes.

125
00:04:17,907 --> 00:04:20,729
Number five, the frosted window.

126
00:04:20,909 --> 00:04:22,861
In the nineteen fifties,

127
00:04:22,861 --> 00:04:26,279
everybody used a product called Glass Wax,

128
00:04:26,279 --> 00:04:29,777
with little cardboard stencils of stars and

129
00:04:29,777 --> 00:04:31,242
bells and snowmen.

130
00:04:31,422 --> 00:04:34,159
You dabbed it on with a sponge,

131
00:04:34,159 --> 00:04:35,837
and it dried white,

132
00:04:35,837 --> 00:04:36,809
like frost.

133
00:04:36,989 --> 00:04:39,328
Today, honey, you can do the same

134
00:04:39,328 --> 00:04:41,951
thing with the canned spray snow from

135
00:04:41,951 --> 00:04:44,503
the dollar store and a paper stencil

136
00:04:44,503 --> 00:04:45,708
you cut yourself.

137
00:04:45,888 --> 00:04:48,314
Just do the bottom corners.

138
00:04:48,494 --> 00:04:50,619
A little goes a long way.

139
00:04:50,968 --> 00:04:53,221
Number six, a silhouette.

140
00:04:53,401 --> 00:04:55,793
Cut a simple shape out of black

141
00:04:55,793 --> 00:04:58,186
construction paper, a nativity,

142
00:04:58,186 --> 00:04:59,884
a sleigh and reindeer,

143
00:04:59,884 --> 00:05:01,274
a row of carolers,

144
00:05:01,274 --> 00:05:03,126
a church with a steeple,

145
00:05:03,126 --> 00:05:05,056
and tape it to the glass,

146
00:05:05,056 --> 00:05:05,905
facing out.

147
00:05:06,085 --> 00:05:07,275
During the day,

148
00:05:07,275 --> 00:05:08,941
you barely notice it.

149
00:05:09,121 --> 00:05:11,587
At night, with a lamp on behind

150
00:05:11,587 --> 00:05:14,293
it, it's a perfect black shadow on

151
00:05:14,293 --> 00:05:15,646
a glowing window.

152
00:05:15,826 --> 00:05:18,262
People will stop on the sidewalk,

153
00:05:18,262 --> 00:05:18,705
honey.

154
00:05:18,885 --> 00:05:20,947
In a minute I'll tell you which

155
00:05:20,947 --> 00:05:22,078
shape is easiest.

156
00:05:22,427 --> 00:05:24,331
The easiest silhouette,

157
00:05:24,331 --> 00:05:27,640
sweetheart, is a row of evergreen trees,

158
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,882
in three sizes,

159
00:05:28,882 --> 00:05:32,191
standing along the bottom of the window.

160
00:05:32,371 --> 00:05:35,053
Just triangles, with a little trunk.

161
00:05:35,233 --> 00:05:37,602
Anybody can cut a triangle.

162
00:05:37,782 --> 00:05:39,356
And if you're braver,

163
00:05:39,356 --> 00:05:41,980
put a little star above the tallest

164
00:05:41,980 --> 00:05:44,678
one, cut out of yellow tissue paper,

165
00:05:44,678 --> 00:05:47,302
so the light comes through it gold.

166
00:05:47,482 --> 00:05:50,068
Danny and I made ours every year,

167
00:05:50,068 --> 00:05:52,733
and his trees always leaned to the

168
00:05:52,733 --> 00:05:53,124
left.

169
00:05:53,474 --> 00:05:57,213
Number seven, a window box of evergreens.

170
00:05:57,393 --> 00:06:00,033
If you have a window box outside,

171
00:06:00,033 --> 00:06:02,754
don't leave it empty and brown all

172
00:06:02,754 --> 00:06:03,314
winter.

173
00:06:03,494 --> 00:06:06,171
Stick in some cut pine and cedar

174
00:06:06,171 --> 00:06:09,100
branches, a few red twigs or holly,

175
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:10,773
and three pinecones.

176
00:06:10,953 --> 00:06:13,670
Push them right into the frozen dirt.

177
00:06:13,850 --> 00:06:16,497
They'll stay green until spring in the

178
00:06:16,497 --> 00:06:16,845
cold.

179
00:06:17,025 --> 00:06:19,146
And if you add a little string

180
00:06:19,146 --> 00:06:22,185
of battery lights tucked into the branches,

181
00:06:22,185 --> 00:06:24,305
it glows from the outside too.

182
00:06:24,654 --> 00:06:27,814
Number eight, a tiny village on the

183
00:06:27,814 --> 00:06:28,265
sill.

184
00:06:28,445 --> 00:06:30,760
A few little ceramic houses,

185
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:33,572
a church, some bottle-brush trees,

186
00:06:33,572 --> 00:06:35,804
a scoop of cotton for snow,

187
00:06:35,804 --> 00:06:38,698
and a string of fairy lights tucked

188
00:06:38,698 --> 00:06:39,277
behind.

189
00:06:39,457 --> 00:06:42,304
Thrift stores are full of those little

190
00:06:42,304 --> 00:06:43,727
houses in November,

191
00:06:43,727 --> 00:06:44,926
a dollar apiece.

192
00:06:45,106 --> 00:06:48,041
Put the village right on the windowsill,

193
00:06:48,041 --> 00:06:50,536
facing out, so the street sees the

194
00:06:50,536 --> 00:06:52,077
fronts of the houses,

195
00:06:52,077 --> 00:06:53,105
not the backs.

196
00:06:53,285 --> 00:06:55,862
Most people get that backwards.

197
00:06:56,212 --> 00:06:57,232
A bonus one,

198
00:06:57,232 --> 00:07:00,206
sweetheart, for the kitchen window.

199
00:07:00,386 --> 00:07:02,684
Dry a few thin slices of orange

200
00:07:02,684 --> 00:07:03,721
in a low oven,

201
00:07:03,721 --> 00:07:06,834
two hundred degrees for about three hours,

202
00:07:06,834 --> 00:07:09,279
until they're like stained glass.

203
00:07:09,459 --> 00:07:11,722
Tie them on red thread and hang

204
00:07:11,722 --> 00:07:14,057
them in a row across the window.

205
00:07:14,237 --> 00:07:16,590
When the sun comes through in the

206
00:07:16,590 --> 00:07:19,086
morning, they glow orange and gold,

207
00:07:19,086 --> 00:07:21,795
and the kitchen smells like Christmas.

208
00:07:21,975 --> 00:07:24,065
Cost of three oranges.

209
00:07:24,245 --> 00:07:26,671
My mother called them sun ornaments.

210
00:07:27,021 --> 00:07:28,530
So far, candles,

211
00:07:28,530 --> 00:07:30,510
snowflakes, a wreath,

212
00:07:30,510 --> 00:07:32,961
floating ornaments, frost,

213
00:07:32,961 --> 00:07:35,412
silhouettes, a window box,

214
00:07:35,412 --> 00:07:37,203
and a tiny village.

215
00:07:37,383 --> 00:07:39,508
Now, the three mistakes.

216
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:42,407
Mistake one is the candle in the

217
00:07:42,407 --> 00:07:43,426
wrong place.

218
00:07:43,606 --> 00:07:46,516
People put the window candle off to

219
00:07:46,516 --> 00:07:48,262
one side, on a table.

220
00:07:48,442 --> 00:07:49,539
From the street,

221
00:07:49,539 --> 00:07:51,321
it looks like an accident.

222
00:07:51,501 --> 00:07:53,515
Put it dead center on the sill,

223
00:07:53,515 --> 00:07:55,658
right in the middle of the glass.

224
00:07:55,838 --> 00:07:57,916
Centered looks intentional.

225
00:07:58,096 --> 00:08:00,511
Off to the side looks forgotten.

226
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:03,090
Mistake two is too much.

227
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:06,238
People tape up snowflakes and silhouettes and

228
00:08:06,238 --> 00:08:08,744
stickers and lights and garland all in

229
00:08:08,744 --> 00:08:09,800
the same window,

230
00:08:09,800 --> 00:08:11,845
and from the street it's just a

231
00:08:11,845 --> 00:08:12,174
mess.

232
00:08:12,354 --> 00:08:14,480
One idea per window,

233
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:15,118
honey.

234
00:08:15,298 --> 00:08:17,097
Snowflakes in the kitchen.

235
00:08:17,277 --> 00:08:19,204
Silhouette in the living room.

236
00:08:19,384 --> 00:08:21,254
Candles upstairs.

237
00:08:21,434 --> 00:08:24,046
Let each window tell one story.

238
00:08:24,226 --> 00:08:26,141
Mistake three is cords.

239
00:08:26,321 --> 00:08:29,427
A white extension cord snaking across the

240
00:08:29,427 --> 00:08:31,093
sill ruins everything.

241
00:08:31,273 --> 00:08:32,705
Use battery lights,

242
00:08:32,705 --> 00:08:35,117
or tuck the cord down behind the

243
00:08:35,117 --> 00:08:35,720
curtain.

244
00:08:36,069 --> 00:08:36,988
Stay with me,

245
00:08:36,988 --> 00:08:38,895
because here's number nine,

246
00:08:38,895 --> 00:08:40,237
my mother's window.

247
00:08:40,417 --> 00:08:43,624
Every Christmas, she took the big front

248
00:08:43,624 --> 00:08:45,351
window on Elm Street,

249
00:08:45,351 --> 00:08:48,475
and she pulled the curtains wide open,

250
00:08:48,475 --> 00:08:49,462
all the way.

251
00:08:49,642 --> 00:08:52,147
She set our Christmas tree right in

252
00:08:52,147 --> 00:08:53,006
front of it,

253
00:08:53,006 --> 00:08:54,367
close to the glass.

254
00:08:54,547 --> 00:08:56,888
She turned off every other lamp in

255
00:08:56,888 --> 00:08:57,507
the room.

256
00:08:57,687 --> 00:09:00,033
And she lit only the tree.

257
00:09:00,213 --> 00:09:01,410
Just the tree,

258
00:09:01,410 --> 00:09:03,975
honey, glowing in a dark room,

259
00:09:03,975 --> 00:09:06,711
framed in that big window like a

260
00:09:06,711 --> 00:09:07,480
painting.

261
00:09:07,830 --> 00:09:09,161
And on the sill,

262
00:09:09,161 --> 00:09:10,907
in front of the tree,

263
00:09:10,907 --> 00:09:12,487
she set one candle,

264
00:09:12,487 --> 00:09:15,813
dead center, and our family's old wooden

265
00:09:15,813 --> 00:09:17,560
nativity, facing out.

266
00:09:17,740 --> 00:09:18,866
That was it.

267
00:09:19,046 --> 00:09:21,652
No snowflakes, no stickers,

268
00:09:21,652 --> 00:09:22,714
no garland.

269
00:09:22,894 --> 00:09:25,919
People slowed their cars on Elm Street,

270
00:09:25,919 --> 00:09:26,772
sweetheart.

271
00:09:26,952 --> 00:09:30,110
Children pressed their mittens against the glass.

272
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:32,445
And my mother used to stand in

273
00:09:32,445 --> 00:09:34,241
the dark kitchen doorway,

274
00:09:34,241 --> 00:09:36,252
where they couldn't see her,

275
00:09:36,252 --> 00:09:37,976
and watch them watching.

276
00:09:38,325 --> 00:09:39,663
She told me once,

277
00:09:39,663 --> 00:09:43,283
Ruthie, people decorate for the family inside.

278
00:09:43,463 --> 00:09:46,040
But a window is for the strangers.

279
00:09:46,220 --> 00:09:49,106
Somebody walking by tonight is lonely,

280
00:09:49,106 --> 00:09:51,460
or grieving, or a long way from

281
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:51,840
home.

282
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:53,267
And for one minute,

283
00:09:53,267 --> 00:09:54,974
standing on that sidewalk,

284
00:09:54,974 --> 00:09:57,272
they'll feel like they're part of a

285
00:09:57,272 --> 00:09:57,732
family.

286
00:09:57,912 --> 00:10:00,648
